# Infrastructure (Terraform)

Infrastructure is modular Terraform under `infra/`. The root `infra/main.tf` wires four reusable modules in `infra/modules/`:

- `lambda` — one instance per function (`api`, `cron`, `worker`); zips `apps/<fn>/dist` into the git-ignored `.build/`, creates the function + IAM role.
- `api-gateway` — HTTP API (v2) `$default` route proxying to the `api` Lambda.
- `sqs` — main queue + DLQ, consume policy, and the event source mapping to the `worker` Lambda.
- `eventbridge` — schedule rule + target invoking the `cron` Lambda.

`infra/providers.tf` defines `local.resource_name` (`${project_name}-${env}`) and `local.common_tags`, applied as provider `default_tags` and passed to every module. Root outputs (`api_url`, `queue_url`, `cron_rule_arn`) live in `infra/outputs.tf`. Vars are in `infra/variables.tf` (`project_name`, `env`); state is remote S3 (`infra/backend.tf` + per-env `infra/backend/{dev,prod}.hcl`).

## Profile and region

`infra/backend/{dev,prod}.hcl` hold the `bucket`, the `key` and `use_lockfile` only. No file of `infra/` sets an AWS profile or a region: Terraform reads `AWS_PROFILE` and `AWS_REGION` from the environment, for the S3 backend and the AWS provider alike. That is what lets the same files work on a laptop (a profile) and in CI (the OIDC step exports short-lived credentials and `AWS_REGION`). A `profile` in a backend file would break CI, which has no profile, and a `region` there or in a tfvars would give the region a second source next to the GitHub variable `AWS_REGION`.

## First deploy

The ordered first deploy, from the credentials to the live URL and the GitHub setup, is the "Deploying to AWS (Terraform)" section of `README.md`. The commands live only there.

## Ops (justfile)

Install `just` first (https://github.com/casey/just). Run from repo root; it sets `working-directory := 'infra'` and takes an `env` param (default `dev`):

- `just init <env>` — `terraform init` with the env's backend config.
- `just plan <env>` / `just apply <env>` / `just destroy <env>` — pass `-var="env=<env>"`.
- `just check <env>` — prints the Terraform version, `AWS_PROFILE`, `AWS_REGION` and the number of resources in the state.

## CD (`.github/workflows/deploy.yml`)

Pushes to `main` deploy `dev`; `workflow_dispatch` picks `dev`/`prod`. Auth is OIDC via `aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials` (`secrets.AWS_ROLE_ARN` and `vars.AWS_REGION` of the GitHub environment `dev` or `prod`, `permissions: id-token: write`); the action exports `AWS_REGION`, which the backend and the provider read. It lints (when the project has a linter), builds, then `terraform init -backend-config=backend/<env>.hcl`, plan, apply. The README section sets up the role and the GitHub values.

## Add a Lambda function

1. Create `apps/<name>/` (build to `dist/index.mjs`: Terraform zips only `dist/`, so the ES module bundle loads as `.mjs` only). {{#if (eq pm "bun")}}Build it with `NODE_ENV=production bun build`: `bun build` inlines `process.env.NODE_ENV` into the bundle, `development` by default, and Lambda sets no `NODE_ENV` of its own, so a plain build ships a development bundle (the API error handler would return stack traces).{{else}}Build it with `esbuild` and `--define:process.env.NODE_ENV=\"production\"` (copy the `build` script of `apps/api`, whose backslashes keep the quotes through the shell): Lambda sets no `NODE_ENV` of its own, so a bundle that keeps reading it ships in development mode (the API error handler would return stack traces).{{/if}}
2. Add a `module "<name>"` block in `infra/main.tf` using `source = "./modules/lambda"`.
3. Wire any trigger (new `api-gateway`/`sqs`/`eventbridge` module call) and expose outputs in `infra/outputs.tf`.
